  "worked_solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+k[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ k[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the first components, we get \n\n$$\n3k=-6\\,\u27f9\\,k=-2.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $3{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$",
